      "text": "We start with the given functional equation:\n\\[f(x) + 2f(1 - x) = 3x^2.\\]\nWe want to find the value of \\(f(3)\\). To do this, we will substitute \\(x = 3\\) into the equation:\n\\[f(3) + 2f(1 - 3) = 3(3)^2.\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[f(3) + 2f(-2) = 27.\\]\nNext, we substitute \\(x = -2\\) into the original equation:\n\\[f(-2) + 2f(1 - (-2)) = 3(-2)^2.\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[f(-2) + 2f(3) = 12.\\]\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n1. \\(f(3) + 2f(-2) = 27,\\)\n2. \\(f(-2) + 2f(3) = 12.\\)\nWe can solve this system. First, we will solve the first equation for \\(f(-2)\\):\n\\[f(-2) = \\frac{27 - f(3)}{2}.\\]\nNext, we substitute this expression for \\(f(-2)\\) into the second equation:\n\\[\\frac{27 - f(3)}{2} + 2f(3) = 12.\\]\nTo clear the fraction, we multiply every term by 2:\n\\[27 - f(3) + 4f(3) = 24.\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[27 + 3f(3) = 24.\\]\nSubtracting 27 from both sides, we get:\n\\[3f(3) = -3.\\]\nDividing both sides by 3, we find:\n\\[f(3) = -1.\\]\nThus, the value of \\(f(3)\\) is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\).",
