      "text": "Given the equation:\n\\[\nf(x) + 2f(1 - x) = 3x^2\n\\]\nfor all real numbers \\(x\\), we need to find \\(f(3)\\).\n\nFirst, let's substitute \\(x = 3\\) into the given equation:\n\\[\nf(3) + 2f(1 - 3) = 3(3)^2\n\\]\nSimplify the right-hand side:\n\\[\nf(3) + 2f(-2) = 27\n\\]\nSo, we have:\n\\[\nf(3) + 2f(-2) = 27 \\quad \\text{(1)}\n\\]\n\nNext, let's substitute \\(x = -2\\) into the given equation:\n\\[\nf(-2) + 2f(1 - (-2)) = 3(-2)^2\n\\]\nSimplify the right-hand side:\n\\[\nf(-2) + 2f(3) = 12\n\\]\nSo, we have:\n\\[\nf(-2) + 2f(3) = 12 \\quad \\text{(2)}\n\\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n1. \\(f(3) + 2f(-2) = 27\\)\n2. \\(f(-2) + 2f(3) = 12\\)\n\nTo solve this system, we can eliminate one of the variables. Let's multiply the first equation by 2:\n\\[\n2f(3) + 4f(-2) = 54 \\quad \\text{(3)}\n\\]\n\nNow, we subtract the second equation from equation (3):\n\\[\n(2f(3) + 4f(-2)) - (f(-2) + 2f(3)) = 54 - 12\n\\]\nSimplify:\n\\[\n2f(3) + 4f(-2) - f(-2) - 2f(3) = 42\n\\]\n\\[\n3f(-2) = 42\n\\]\nSolve for \\(f(-2)\\):\n\\[\nf(-2) = \\frac{42}{3} = 14\n\\]\n\nNow substitute \\(f(-2) = 14\\) back into equation (2):\n\\[\n14 + 2f(3) = 12\n\\]\nSolve for \\(f(3)\\):\n\\[\n2f(3) = 12 - 14\n\\]\n\\[\n2f(3) = -2\n\\]\n\\[\nf(3) = \\frac{-2}{2} = -1\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(f(3)\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-1}\n\\]",
