      "text": "To solve for \\( f(3) \\), we first need to find the expression for \\( f(x) \\) using the given functional equation:\n\\[ f(x) + 2f(1 - x) = 3x^2. \\]\n\nWe start by substituting \\( x = 3 \\) into the equation:\n\\[ f(3) + 2f(1 - 3) = 3(3)^2. \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ f(3) + 2f(-2) = 27. \\]\n\nNext, we substitute \\( x = -2 \\) into the original equation:\n\\[ f(-2) + 2f(1 - (-2)) = 3(-2)^2. \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ f(-2) + 2f(3) = 12. \\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n1. \\( f(3) + 2f(-2) = 27, \\)\n2. \\( f(-2) + 2f(3) = 12. \\)\n\nLet's denote \\( f(3) \\) by \\( a \\) and \\( f(-2) \\) by \\( b \\). The system of equations becomes:\n\\[ a + 2b = 27, \\]\n\\[ b + 2a = 12. \\]\n\nWe can solve this system by eliminating one of the variables. First, we multiply the second equation by 2:\n\\[ 2b + 4a = 24. \\]\n\nNow we subtract the first equation from this new equation:\n\\[ (2b + 4a) - (a + 2b) = 24 - 27, \\]\nwhich simplifies to:\n\\[ 3a = -3. \\]\nThus, we have:\n\\[ a = -1. \\]\n\nSubstituting \\( a = -1 \\) back into the first equation:\n\\[ -1 + 2b = 27, \\]\nwe get:\n\\[ 2b = 28, \\]\nso:\n\\[ b = 14. \\]\n\nTherefore, \\( f(3) = a = -1 \\). The final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{-1}. \\]",
